WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.200
If you're trying to build a consistent
AI influencer and every single image

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.680
looks like a completely different girl,
this is exactly the video you need.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.200
I'm going to show you the exact workflow
I use to train one

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.920
stable character that stays the same
by training a hyper realistic LoRa.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:15.480
And if you

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.400
watch till the end of the video,
I will show you

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.640
how to get the whole exact workflow
I use complete for free.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:21.800
The workflow is simple.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.480
We don't start with training,
we start with the data set.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.000
Most people
want to jump straight into training,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.160
but a LoRa is only as good
as the data set under it for Ashley, I'm

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.240
using 34 images of the same character
from different angles and poses,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.560
so the model only learns one identity
and nothing else.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.880
And here's the part
where most people go wrong.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.040
They just train on a raw data set.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:43.920
I mix it up.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.640
Some are clean for the sharp detail,

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.120
and some are blurry phone style shots
for the hyper realistic look.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.440
The more types of images you feed
in, the more flexibility the LoRa gets.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.760
If you want to go fully Pro mode,
you train a separate

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.840
LoRa for separate jobs, for example
one for selfies,

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.640
one for NSFW, and so on for one solid
all round character.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:04.840
This mix is enough.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.160
Once the data set is ready,
we take it straight into RunPod

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.280
and start the training.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.200
And if you want to see how I build Ashley

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.120
from scratch, the face and the base
images, then go watch my last video.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.360
You can also find data set prompts
for free in my discord channel.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.600
For the training we jump over to RunPod.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.600
And to be honest,
this is the easiest part ever.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.920
You just rent a GPU,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.920
let it run for about an hour and a half
and you're out for under five bucks.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.120
We spin up our new pod and grab the RTX
5090.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.880
Don't overthink the GPU here.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.080
This one is more than enough
for what we are doing.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.160
Then we swap the template search
AI toolkit and take the verified one.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.480
Give the volume disk 150 gigs.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.680
So we've got room for the checkpoints
and click Deploy on Demand.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.040
Give it a minute to boot.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.520
Then over in the connect tab,
the access pops up and we open the HTTP

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.800
service link that drops us straight
inside the AI toolkit.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.320
And the password is, by the way,
just literally only password.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.000
Inside we go to the Data set tab,
create a new data set and name it.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.120
Then we drag in all 34 images
and let it upload.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.840
Now our data set is in
and we set up the training.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.440
After that
we create a new job and name our LoRa.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.280
For the trigger word.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.600
We use one unique word,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.560
something the model doesn't already know,
so it ties only to our LoRa.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:16.280
Newer models

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.680
don't really need a trigger word anymore,
but I still like to keep one in.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.200
Then we set the model architecture
to the Z-Image-turbo, and also set

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.520
the max step input to 30
to speed up the training a bit.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.600
And by the way,
let me know in the comments

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.040
what kind of lower training videos
you want to see.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.080
And when we done this, we keep the steps
at 3000 for efficient Z-Image-turbo Lora.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.360
That part matters because you don't want
to over train or overfit a turbo model.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.440
Make also sure

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.520
the right data set is selected and tell it
to save the checkpoints along the way

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.840
so we can pick the best one later
instead of just trusting the last turn.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.560
Also off in the resolution section
512 and 768,

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.200
because we only want the best quality, and
that's the reason why we keep only 1080.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.720
Then turn on, skip the first sample
and keep the last three prompts.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.840
After we set it everything up, we hit
create job, press play and it's on it.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:06.240
It download

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.720
a few files, gives you an estimate
and runs for about an hour and a half.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.400
Once the training is done,
we go through the checkpoints.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.120
I don't know the last 3 to 5

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.720
LoRa's to have a good selection
where I can pick the best LoRa out of it.

